---
const meanings = [
  ["fair", "an event, just, or light in colour"],
  ["right", "a direction, correct, or an entitlement"],
  ["bark", "the covering of a tree, or the sound a dog makes"]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Meaning in Context - The sentence decides</h1>
  <section class="summary-block summary-yellow">
    <h2>One word, this sentence</h2>
    <p>“He broke the record.” Here <em>record</em> means an achievement.</p>
    <p>“He played the record.” Here <em>record</em> means a music disc.</p>
    <p>“Choose the meaning of <em>present</em> in this sentence.” She received a <em>present</em> from her friend, so <em>present</em> means a <strong>gift</strong>.</p>
    <p>Reread the sentence. The meaning depends on this context, not on the first meaning you remember.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-lilac">
    <h2>Several meanings</h2>
    <p>Some words have more than one meaning. The sentence shows which one is in use.</p>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Word</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Meanings</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          meanings.map(([word, senses]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{word}</th>
              <td>{senses}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>“Which sentence uses <em>bark</em> to mean a sound?” Read the whole sentence before you decide.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-green">
    <h2>Clues beside an unfamiliar word</h2>
    <p>“The tiny kitten trembled in the cold breeze.” <em>Trembled</em> means <strong>shook</strong> or <strong>shivered</strong>.</p>
    <p>“Which word means the same as <em>trembled</em> in this sentence?”</p>
    <p>Look for a synonym near the word, an opposite after <em>but</em> or <em>although</em>, or an example or definition inside the sentence.</p>
  </section>
</div>
